1
00:00:00,620 --> 00:00:07,200
Greetings and welcome to this MOOC, Introduction to Biorisk Management.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:13,660
This is the second lecture and in this lecture I will be introducing you to the Biorisk Management

3
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:23,180
System.

4
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:30,820
The effectiveness and efficiency of a laboratory Biorisk Management System is based on identifying,

5
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:37,340
understanding and managing a system of interrelated processes for a given objective.

6
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:43,720
This will involve defining, structuring and understanding the system as well as continually

7
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:50,380
improving the system through management and evaluation as well as establishing resources

8
00:00:50,380 --> 00:00:55,170
constraints prior to action.

9
00:00:55,170 --> 00:01:02,290
Now as an effective biosafety manager you have to understand that the approach to laboratory

10
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:07,970
management involves a knowledge of multiple disciplines.

11
00:01:07,970 --> 00:01:16,590
You have to acquire a knowledge of the life sciences, a knowledge of legislation or laws

12
00:01:16,590 --> 00:01:22,370
in your respective country pertaining to biosafety and biosecurity as well as some knowledge

13
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:24,130
of engineering.

14
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:29,890
You should be able to understand some engineering concepts such as directional air flow and

15
00:01:29,890 --> 00:01:37,890
air conditioning systems as well as plumbing and other architectural elements.

16
00:01:37,890 --> 00:01:46,130
This is something that you need to acquire over the period of your occupation as a biosafety

17
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:51,640
officer or a Biorisk Manager.

18
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:56,520
The learning objectives of this module are to introduce you to the Biorisk Management

19
00:01:56,520 --> 00:02:03,480
System, the Biorisk Policy, Planning, Implementation and Review.

20
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:13,460
These form the essential elements of almost all management practices.

21
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:18,300
The learning outcomes for this particular module are as follows.

22
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:25,060
You should be able to describe the Biorisk Management System, develop a policy statement

23
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:31,980
that defines your organization, develop a plan for the management of your specific objectives,

24
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:37,980
develop a strategy to implement your management system and describe the process of review

25
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,620
and corrective action.

26
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:51,340
Now I understand that not all of you may be employed in a facility that conducts experiments

27
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:53,500
involving biological agents.

28
00:02:53,500 --> 00:03:01,180
However, for the purpose of this MOOC, I want you to construct a virtual environment

29
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:04,060
for you.

30
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:10,020
Develop a policy statement for a laboratory management system which you have envisioned.

31
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:16,300
Develop a plan, develop a strategy to implement this plan and describe how you will review

32
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:24,330
your implementation and how you will apply corrective action.

33
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:33,450
In Biorisk Management, we adopt what is known as a management systems approach.

34
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:37,730
And what exactly is this management system approach?

35
00:03:37,730 --> 00:03:45,570
I will delve into this with an example, but for now I will describe some of the elements.

36
00:03:45,570 --> 00:03:52,810
The first thing that you need to do as a Biorisk Manager is to define your system.

37
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:57,010
Once you have defined your system, you can structure that system.

38
00:03:57,010 --> 00:04:06,050
After you structure it, you can understand the system in terms of interrelated functionalities.

39
00:04:06,050 --> 00:04:09,930
Then you move on to continually improving the system.

40
00:04:09,930 --> 00:04:17,110
And finally, you establish your resource constraints.

41
00:04:17,110 --> 00:04:22,310
All Biorisk Management is founded on a policy.

42
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:31,350
It is essential for an organization to develop a policy statement that caters specifically

43
00:04:31,350 --> 00:04:33,870
to Biorisk Management.

44
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:40,790
For instance, this is an example of a policy in which I have stated a generic word the

45
00:04:40,790 --> 00:04:43,550
organization is committed.

46
00:04:43,550 --> 00:04:49,710
This is an essential word in every policy to its core business.

47
00:04:49,710 --> 00:04:55,790
You can replace this word core business with a specific operation associated with your

48
00:04:55,790 --> 00:04:59,470
particular organization.

49
00:04:59,470 --> 00:05:04,750
The next word which I have stated here is continuous improvement.

50
00:05:04,750 --> 00:05:13,470
This is the element which ensures that your Biorisk Management system is constantly improved

51
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:18,230
upon throughout its life cycle.

52
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:25,030
And finally, I have included a word which is compliance.

53
00:05:25,030 --> 00:05:30,310
compliance with international and national guidelines and legislation.

54
00:05:30,310 --> 00:05:33,310
This is basically a generic policy.

55
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:43,060
However, you can adopt this policy to your respective organization.

56
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:47,260
This is a very well-known concept in management.

57
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:50,460
The concept of continuous improvement.

58
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:58,700
It involves a cyclical process of planning, doing or implementing, checking and acting

59
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:03,220
to improve upon the original plan.

60
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:11,700
And this leads to a continuous improvement of quality.

61
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:14,700
Coming down to an actual example.

62
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:22,940
Let us say all of us are involved in an organization and we plan to set up a diagnostic

63
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:26,140
laboratory for detection of viruses.

64
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:31,190
Let us take this as a real example.

65
00:06:31,190 --> 00:06:39,990
So as a Biorisk Manager, what are the questions which you will pose to your management team?

66
00:06:39,990 --> 00:06:45,630
The first question is what are the laboratory procedures involved?

67
00:06:45,630 --> 00:06:49,310
What is the instrumentation required?

68
00:06:49,310 --> 00:06:53,150
And what are the standard operating procedures?

69
00:06:53,150 --> 00:06:57,510
The second question is how do we organize the procedures?

70
00:06:57,510 --> 00:07:03,710
Organization of procedures involves planning of the work as well as planning of the allocation

71
00:07:03,710 --> 00:07:06,030
of tasks to personnel.

72
00:07:06,030 --> 00:07:11,830
The third question is are multiple procedures involved?

73
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:18,230
Handling of samples derived from patients may involve multiple procedures such as transport,

74
00:07:18,230 --> 00:07:22,030
decontamination or inactivation.

75
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:30,790
And disposal of waste is another process which may be linked to the operation of your laboratory.

76
00:07:30,790 --> 00:07:38,110
The next question we ask is how can I improve the system in terms of safety and efficiency?

77
00:07:38,110 --> 00:07:44,590
Now as a Biorisk Manager, you will note that during the operation of your system,

78
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:49,070
you will encounter what are known as accidents and incidents.

79
00:07:49,070 --> 00:07:58,750
And accidents and incidents are an indication of some gap in your operational guidelines.

80
00:07:58,750 --> 00:08:08,710
These need to be addressed before they can end up as serious incidents or serious accidents.

81
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:13,550
And this forms the basis for continuous quality improvement.

82
00:08:14,110 --> 00:08:19,670
Finally, the last question which you must address is constraints.

83
00:08:19,670 --> 00:08:20,670
What are your constraints?

84
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:23,350
Do you have any financial constraints?

85
00:08:23,350 --> 00:08:29,070
Do you have constraints in terms of qualified and competent personnel?

86
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:36,390
These are the questions which you must address when you want to commence the planning of

87
00:08:36,390 --> 00:08:44,910
any procedure or operation involving biological agents.

88
00:08:44,910 --> 00:08:51,350
Let us look at the same case in point by analyzing the process.

89
00:08:51,350 --> 00:08:59,670
So the process of viral diagnosis and detection involves with the sample itself followed by

90
00:08:59,670 --> 00:09:03,910
culture of the sample in the laboratory.

91
00:09:03,910 --> 00:09:10,470
This may then involve examination using either microscopy or other techniques such as ELISA

92
00:09:10,470 --> 00:09:11,470
or PCR.

93
00:09:12,470 --> 00:09:16,870
Finally, the results have to be documented.

94
00:09:16,870 --> 00:09:26,510
And these results may have to be uploaded to a server or to the client using a secure protocol.

95
00:09:26,510 --> 00:09:32,630
All of these procedures may result in the generation of waste.

96
00:09:32,630 --> 00:09:41,510
And this waste needs to be decontaminated and disposed of in compliance with regulations

97
00:09:41,510 --> 00:09:44,750
and standard operating procedures.

98
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:53,110
Now this is an example of multiple processes in a single organization or even for a single

99
00:09:53,110 --> 00:09:55,950
diagnostic procedure.

100
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:59,830
Now the next layer involves personnel.

101
00:09:59,830 --> 00:10:04,270
Each stage of this process involves personnel.

102
00:10:04,270 --> 00:10:11,230
And these personnel have to be trained to address the risk posed by each of these procedures.

103
00:10:11,230 --> 00:10:17,950
For instance, the personnel handling the sample itself and inactivating the sample may have

104
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:22,430
to be trained in a specific standard operating procedure.

105
00:10:22,430 --> 00:10:29,510
So will the person who is culturing the sample and examining the sample.

106
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:39,070
The waste disposal procedures have to be imparted to the personnel involved in the disposal

107
00:10:39,070 --> 00:10:44,960
of waste.

108
00:10:44,960 --> 00:10:51,220
This brings us to the step by step process of laboratory management.

109
00:10:51,220 --> 00:10:57,780
The first step involves defining the system in which you establish objectives and define

110
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:03,740
processes just as we have done in this example.

111
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:07,780
The next step involves structuring the system.

112
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:13,020
Structuring the system involves defining the roles and responsibilities of the various

113
00:11:13,020 --> 00:11:20,620
personnel and the standard operating procedures which they will adopt in order to ensure

114
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:28,280
compliance with the standard operating procedures.

115
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:32,200
The third aspect involves understanding the system.

116
00:11:32,200 --> 00:11:43,040
This involves an understanding of the personnel themselves and defining a specific work plan.

117
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:49,800
A laboratory manager has to be aware of the limitations of personnel.

118
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:58,640
Public and continuity of working operations can lead to accidents and the virus manager

119
00:11:58,640 --> 00:12:11,380
must ensure that workers are properly trained and not taxed beyond the operational capacity.

120
00:12:11,380 --> 00:12:16,980
The next step involves the continuous improvement of the system itself.

121
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:20,940
A virus manager is alert to accidents and incidents.

122
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:30,780
He or she addresses these accidents and incidents, identifies gaps and addresses these gaps by

123
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:41,490
adopting suitable interventions via standard operating procedures.

124
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:47,850
A good virus manager always establishes resource constraints.

125
00:12:47,850 --> 00:12:56,410
Finding a virus management facility is expensive and involves considerable costs.

126
00:12:56,410 --> 00:13:01,770
These may be a constraint to its efficient operation.

127
00:13:01,770 --> 00:13:10,890
Constraints may also come in the form of lack of training and a lack of suitably qualified

128
00:13:10,890 --> 00:13:18,040
or competent personnel.

129
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:24,200
This brings us to the keys to a successful BRM system.

130
00:13:24,200 --> 00:13:28,480
BRM refers to the Biosrisk Management System.

131
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:33,060
Let us look at some of the keys.

132
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:42,460
At the base of this BRM system is the commitment by top management.

133
00:13:42,460 --> 00:13:49,200
Top management must assume ownership of the Biosrisk policy.

134
00:13:49,200 --> 00:13:59,230
They must provide adequate resources to ensure that this policy can be implemented.

135
00:13:59,230 --> 00:14:04,510
Biosrisk management must be prioritized in every organization.

136
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:11,630
Biosrisk policy must be communicated to every member of the organization, including the support

137
00:14:11,630 --> 00:14:20,920
staff as well as disposal staff and cleaners.

138
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:29,120
Biosafety and biosecurity management must be integrated throughout the organization.

139
00:14:29,120 --> 00:14:38,120
This leads us to the process of air-culturation or developing a culture of conscious Biosrisk

140
00:14:38,120 --> 00:14:41,880
management across the organization.

141
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:49,200
The management should always be alert to identifying opportunities for improvement.

142
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:56,440
And in the case of accidents and incidents, the Biosrisk manager must determine root causes

143
00:14:56,440 --> 00:15:03,190
and apply the pertinent corrective action.

144
00:15:03,190 --> 00:15:11,110
The focus of every organization which is involved in the management of the risks associated

145
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:15,170
with biological agents should be on continual improvement.

146
00:15:15,170 --> 00:15:24,490
This is via ownership, periodic assessment, ensuring effectiveness and efficiency of standard

147
00:15:24,490 --> 00:15:32,790
operating procedures, promoting preventing actions, focusing on continuous education and training,

148
00:15:32,790 --> 00:15:42,910
setting goals for improvement, recognizing and rewarding employees.

149
00:15:42,910 --> 00:15:48,710
Let us move on now to the planning phase.

150
00:15:48,710 --> 00:15:54,790
Planning involves risk assessment, hazard identification and risk management.

151
00:15:54,790 --> 00:16:01,950
This constitutes the basis of the AMP cycle which is based on risk assessment, risk mitigation

152
00:16:01,950 --> 00:16:03,850
and performance assessment.

153
00:16:03,850 --> 00:16:14,950
I have discussed this cycle in the first lecture of this MOOC.

154
00:16:14,950 --> 00:16:22,320
Another aspect which is involved in planning is conformity and compliance.

155
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:29,320
You must identify all relevant requirements and fulfill them within the scope of the

156
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:31,320
Biosrisk management system.

157
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:36,920
There may be specific legal requirements which are dependent on your national, federal,

158
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:41,920
state, regional or regulatory authorities.

159
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:50,720
Ensure that your organization complies with these requirements.

160
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:56,640
Planning also involves setting objectives, targets and programs.

161
00:16:56,640 --> 00:17:03,980
One needs to establish, maintain and document virus controls objectives and targets.

162
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:13,490
Documentation is essential in any laboratory as it ensures traceability.

163
00:17:13,490 --> 00:17:20,470
One needs to establish controls and establish procedures for the documentation of the controls

164
00:17:20,470 --> 00:17:29,570
in order to ensure the effective application to the risk management cycle.

165
00:17:29,570 --> 00:17:33,670
Let us move on to implementation and operation.

166
00:17:33,670 --> 00:17:41,630
The first stage of a Biosrisk management system involves planning.

167
00:17:41,690 --> 00:17:51,730
And to that, we implement this plan and operationalize it.

168
00:17:51,730 --> 00:17:57,650
Implementation and operation involves the establishment of the organizational structure,

169
00:17:57,650 --> 00:18:03,170
the allocation of roles and responsibilities to every member of the organization, the

170
00:18:03,170 --> 00:18:09,690
appointment of qualified and competent personnel, the implementation of training as a component

171
00:18:09,710 --> 00:18:16,890
of continuous quality improvement and communication of the Biosrisk management policies and practices

172
00:18:16,890 --> 00:18:21,700
across the organization.

173
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:28,780
This is a brief description of the organizational structure.

174
00:18:28,780 --> 00:18:35,100
At the top of this hierarchy, one notes the top management.

175
00:18:35,100 --> 00:18:41,380
Top management defines the overall policy.

176
00:18:41,380 --> 00:18:47,000
Then we have senior management which reports to top management and the Biosrisk management

177
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:54,220
committee which refers to or reports to the senior management.

178
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:58,420
At the base of this organization are the advisors.

179
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:05,220
The organization may have to call in special advisors to address specific requirements in

180
00:19:05,220 --> 00:19:08,940
terms of biosafety and biosecurity.

181
00:19:08,940 --> 00:19:18,480
The Biosrisk manager or the biosafety officer is a critical component of this hierarchy.

182
00:19:18,480 --> 00:19:20,600
Then we have the facility manager.

183
00:19:20,600 --> 00:19:27,780
The facility manager is an engineer who oversees the management of the facility.

184
00:19:27,780 --> 00:19:36,760
He or she will ensure the operation of the electrical, mechanical, heating, ventilation and

185
00:19:36,760 --> 00:19:40,370
air conditioning systems.

186
00:19:40,370 --> 00:19:50,050
The occupational health and safety personnel are involved in ensuring that the personnel

187
00:19:50,050 --> 00:19:57,130
comply with the OS and H guidelines.

188
00:19:57,130 --> 00:20:06,980
Security management involves both physical security as well as data security.

189
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:15,320
And finally, some facilities which are involved in managing animals and animal studies may

190
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:23,260
have separate personnel to manage animals and their containment facilities.

191
00:20:23,260 --> 00:20:30,860
This is an overview of the structure of the organization.

192
00:20:30,860 --> 00:20:34,700
We now move on to operational management.

193
00:20:34,720 --> 00:20:42,320
Once you have defined the roles and responsibilities of all the personnel involved in the safe working

194
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:49,720
operation of your facility, you have to define the different operations which occur concurrently

195
00:20:49,720 --> 00:20:52,040
in the facility itself.

196
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:55,840
And this involves operational control.

197
00:20:55,840 --> 00:21:02,760
In addition to biosafety, laboratories have to comply with procedures involving electrical,

198
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:06,580
chemical and physical safety.

199
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:16,500
Inventory management is another aspect which is very relevant to documentation of procedures.

200
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:22,740
Work planning must be conducted in order to ensure that work is distributed evenly and

201
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:27,700
personnel are not taxed beyond their operational limits.

202
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:33,800
Personal practices need to be put in place which define the scope and responsibilities

203
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:37,720
of every member of the organization.

204
00:21:37,720 --> 00:21:44,280
Operational management also involves waste management for the safe disposal of waste

205
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:47,680
generated by a facility.

206
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:54,360
Personnel management focuses on the training of personnel in terms of their knowledge,

207
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:58,460
skills and abilities pertaining to biorisk management.

208
00:21:58,460 --> 00:22:04,580
And finally, infrastructure and operational management focuses on the maintenance of the

209
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:13,780
facility itself and involves both preventive maintenance as well as maintenance following

210
00:22:13,780 --> 00:22:16,520
breakdowns.

211
00:22:16,520 --> 00:22:24,860
As you can see, the effective management of any facility involves multiple operations.

212
00:22:24,860 --> 00:22:33,240
These operations will occur concurrently and integrating all these operations is a skill

213
00:22:33,240 --> 00:22:37,240
that must be acquired by a biorisk manager.

214
00:22:37,240 --> 00:22:47,340
Finally, we move on to review.

215
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:55,240
The top management must review the organization's biorisk management system and the review must

216
00:22:55,240 --> 00:22:57,700
be done periodically.

217
00:22:57,700 --> 00:23:03,840
The top management must decide on the periodicity of the review.

218
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:11,380
This may be in the form of an annual review, a weekly review or a monthly review.

219
00:23:11,380 --> 00:23:18,400
The primary reason for this review is to evaluate suitability, adequacy and effectiveness

220
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:28,480
of the risk mitigation measures and to assess opportunities for improvement as part of the

221
00:23:28,480 --> 00:23:34,360
philosophy of continuous quality improvement.

222
00:23:34,360 --> 00:23:41,540
This is some of the information which may be required during a review.

223
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:47,440
One is to look at the results of the audits, evidence of compliance to standard operating

224
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:58,000
procedures, status of risk assessment activities, status of preventive and corrective action,

225
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:05,900
follow up actions from previous management reviews, recommendations for improvement, accident

226
00:24:05,900 --> 00:24:09,400
and incident investigation.

227
00:24:09,400 --> 00:24:16,880
Please take note that documentation at every stage of the operation is critical to an

228
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:24,880
effective and efficient review.

229
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:28,680
This brings us to the end of this lecture.

230
00:24:28,680 --> 00:24:33,880
In this lecture, I have provided you with an overview of the following.

231
00:24:33,880 --> 00:24:41,530
Firstly, we have looked at the management systems approach to biorisk management.

232
00:24:41,530 --> 00:24:47,810
Then we have looked at the key elements of a successful biorisk management system.

233
00:24:47,810 --> 00:24:55,670
We have learned how to develop a biorisk management plan, ensure conformity and compliance

234
00:24:55,670 --> 00:25:05,910
with existing national regulations, implement the biorisk management system and the process

235
00:25:05,910 --> 00:25:10,080
of review of the system.

236
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:18,320
Now in your own specific country, I want you to identify documents related to biosafety

237
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:19,320
and biosecurity.

238
00:25:19,320 --> 00:25:26,360
These can be in the form of laws, guidelines, acts or legislation.

239
00:25:26,360 --> 00:25:35,460
Please refer to these documents as they will be critical to development of effective laboratory

240
00:25:35,460 --> 00:25:38,340
management system.

241
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:40,420
These are some of the references.

